# Naming

Names must convey intent and align with the values or types they hold. Before locking in a name, trace what the symbol is for, where it is used, and how it relates to neighbouring types, routes, or APIs.

## Naming workflow

When you introduce a new name (new export, shared type, module, or cross-app mirror):

- State which source you matched (existing routes, types, UI wording, or API fields).
- Add a short note on why that name fits that source.

## NAME-01 — Choose descriptive and unambiguous names

Single-letter or abbreviated names are prohibited outside loop indices. Use complete words that clearly describe the entity.

```
// bad
fn(q, e)

// good
fn(question, error)
```

## NAME-02 — Make meaningful distinctions

Do not use noise words (`data`, `info`, `manager`, `handler`, `processor`) as the only differentiator between two names. Every name must communicate what makes it distinct.

```
// bad — what is the difference?
getUserData()
getUserInfo()

// good — distinction is explicit
getUserProfile()
getUserPermissions()
```

## NAME-03 — Use pronounceable names

Choose names that can be spoken aloud. Abbreviations that cannot be pronounced create a barrier in code reviews and conversations.

```
// bad
genDtTmpStmp()

// good
generateDateTimeTimestamp()
```

## NAME-04 — Use searchable names

Single-letter names and bare numeric literals cannot be searched for across a codebase. Prefer named symbols.

```
// bad — what does 7 mean? where do you search for it?
for i in 0..7 { ... }

// good
MAX_RETRIES = 7
for attempt in 0..MAX_RETRIES { ... }
```

## NAME-05 — Replace magic literals with named constants

Do not inline domain-meaningful strings or numbers as literals. Give each one an authoritative named constant so meaning is obvious at the definition site and refactors change one place.

Exceptions that are fine as literals: UI display text, error messages, log strings, single-character punctuation.

```
// bad
if spec.length == 2 { ... }
scheme = 'red'

// good
VAS_POLE_COUNT = 2
if spec.length == VAS_POLE_COUNT { ... }
scheme: ColourScheme = ColourScheme.Red   // closed set enforced by type
```

## NAME-06 — Avoid encodings and type prefixes

Do not append type information or prefixes to names (`strName`, `iCount`, `IUserInterface`, `m_field`). The type system and IDE already communicate type; encoding it in the name adds noise and breaks when types change.

```
// bad
strFirstName = "Alice"
IRepository
m_userId

// good
firstName = "Alice"
Repository
userId
```

## NAME-07 — One vocabulary for parallel implementations

When two or more implementations solve the same problem (legacy vs replacement, different runtimes, strategy variants), name the same concept identically everywhere. Distinguish implementations with one stable differentiator — suffix, prefix, or namespace — not synonyms.

```
// bad — same responsibility, different words; reader must mentally translate
fetchUserProfile()   // v1
loadAccountDetails() // v2

// good — one vocabulary, one explicit differentiator
fetchUserProfileLegacy()
fetchUserProfile()
```

## NAME-08 — Match parameter names to caller identifiers

When a function parameter directly receives a value from a named variable at the call site, use the same identifier. Do not silently rename between styles (e.g. snake_case to camelCase).

```
// bad — caller has response_nrl; renaming creates a silent remapping
validate(responseNrl: response_nrl)

// good — name matches the call-site identifier
validate(response_nrl: response_nrl)
// or simply
validate({ response_nrl })
```
